Fix the font fallback[] index skew, and bounds-check the fallback sprite bank.

FontWithFace::render() builds three parallel arrays for the glyphs it will draw.
Two of them are COMPACT -- `indices` via emplace_back() and `offsets` via
push_back() -- but `fallback` was sized to gl.size() and written at the loop
index `i`:

    std::vector<bool> fallback(text_size);
    ...
    fallback[i] = use_fallback_font;

The collect loop `continue`s on GLF_NEWLINE before appending to either compact
array, so as soon as the text contains one newline the loop index and the
compact index diverge. Both draw loops then read all three with the COMPACT
index n:

    const int sprite_id = indices[n].first;
    if (!fallback[n] && (sprite_id < 0 || sprite_id >= sprite_amount))
        continue;

so for multi-line text fallback[n] describes a different glyph than indices[n].

That is not merely cosmetic, because the bounds check is SKIPPED ENTIRELY when
fallback[n] is true. A main-bank sprite_id then indexes the fallback bank, which
holds only the glyphs actually demanded and is normally far smaller:

    (*fallback_sprites)[sprite_id].Frames[0].textureNumber

core::array::operator[] has only _IRR_DEBUG_BREAK_IF, so under NDEBUG there is
no check at all -- the same shape as the drive-graph defect fixed in 0014/0015.
The out-of-range read yields a garbage textureNumber, getTexture() returns a
wild video::ITexture*, and FontDrawer::addGlyph()/draw() call grab()/drop() on
it, each a 4-byte read-modify-write through that wild pointer.

Two changes:

  * `fallback` becomes compact (reserve + push_back) so it lines up with
    `indices` and `offsets` by construction. The four in-loop reads use the
    local `use_fallback_font` directly, which is what they always meant.
  * both draw loops bound sprite_id against whichever bank they are about to
    index, instead of skipping the check on the fallback path.

Scope note: this is a real defect on its own terms (a wrong glyph scale and a
wrong texture for multi-line text), independent of whether it turns out to be
the writer behind the allocator corruption under investigation.

Detector (Phoenix-local), CORRECTED. A first version checked the bounds in terms
of the *fixed* fallback[], which cannot work: the compaction repairs the index
before the bounds test runs, so such a check can never observe the bug and would
report zero whether or not the defect was live. The detector now keeps a shadow
of the PRE-FIX indexing (fallback_skewed[], written at the glyph loop index
exactly as the buggy code did, never read for rendering) and fires once, as
STK-FONT-OOB, exactly when the OLD code would have skipped the bounds test and
read past the fallback bank. One fire proves the defect was live in that
workload.

Reachability is not hypothetical: BoldFace::init() calls setFallbackFont(regular),
so any bold glyph absent from the bold TTF takes the fallback branch, and the HUD
uses bold text.

